i386 (32-bit x86) integer arithmetic is broken; armv7 (32-bit ARM) is clean
===========================================================================

Summary of the Phase-3 cross-arch validation (bare kernel, release build):

    arch              ptr   build  check-lib  test_arithmetic  unit-suite
    x86-64 / arm64    64    PASS   PASS       PASS             11 pass / 31 fail
    s390x (BE)        64    PASS   PASS       PASS             11 pass / 31 fail
    armv7 (ARM)       32    PASS   PASS       PASS             11 pass / 31 fail
    i386  (x86)       32    PASS   PASS       FAIL             10 pass / 32 fail

i386 is the ONLY arch that diverges. armv7 is ALSO 32-bit yet matches the
64-bit arches exactly — so 32-bit is not the problem per se.

Symptom on i386
---------------

    $ docker run --platform linux/386 <debian-gcc> ./test_arithmetic
    In some function:
    ARITHMETIC ERROR: Integer overflow in (* 100000000 10000)   # 1e12
    EXIT=65

Two independent layers of breakage
----------------------------------

1. Wrong word-width discriminator.
   sources/kernel_types.h selects `word` on `_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64`:

       #ifdef _POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64       # 64-bit branch
       typedef int64_t word;  WORD_MAX=INT64_MAX  TAG_MASK=0xFFFFFFFF  TAGBODY_SHIFT=32
       #else                             # 32-bit branch
       typedef int32_t word;  WORD_MAX=INT32_MAX  TAG_MASK=0x0000FFFF  TAGBODY_SHIFT=16
       #endif

   On Debian this macro is DEFINED on armv7 but NOT on i386 (verified with
   `gcc -E`). So armv7 gets the int64 branch (64-bit fixnums; most-positive-
   fixnum = 9223372036854775807) while i386 gets the int32 branch
   (most-positive-fixnum ~2.1e9), and (* 100000000 10000)=1e12 overflows the
   fixnum with no bare-kernel bignum fallback. `_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64` is a
   POSIX programming-environment macro, NOT a reliable "is this LP64" test —
   it is the wrong discriminator.

2. i386 arithmetic is ALSO wrong with the int64 branch.
   Forcing the int64 branch on i386 (build + tests with
   CPPFLAGS=-D_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64) removes the overflow but test_arithmetic
   still fails — now with 25 WRONG-VALUE assertions, e.g.

       test_arithmetic.c:227  eql(integer_from_word(1000000000000L), (* 10000 10000 10000))
       test_arithmetic.c:240  eql(integer_from_word(1), (min 3 1 4 1 5 9 2 6))

   i.e. the multiplication/comparison produce wrong results. The SAME int64
   source is correct on armv7 and on all 64-bit arches, so this is an
   i386/x86-32-specific defect (likely undefined behaviour or a 64-bit-int /
   pointer-cast assumption that x86-32 codegen exposes and arm-32 does not).

Conclusion / recommendation
---------------------------

  - armv7 demonstrates the kernel is correct on a 32-bit target, so full
    32-bit support is achievable.
  - i386 needs two fixes, in order:
      a. Replace the `_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64` discriminator with a reliable one
         (e.g. based on UINTPTR_MAX / __SIZEOF_POINTER__, or just always use
         int64 `word`), so i386 also gets 64-bit fixnums like armv7.
      b. Then chase the residual i386-only wrong-arithmetic failures under a
         sanitiser build (UBSan: -fsanitize=undefined) on i386 — they smell
         like UB in the 64-bit integer / tagged-word handling.
  - Until then, i386 is not a shippable target; armv7, s390x, x86-64, arm64
    are clean. Treat i386 as a separate work item, not a CI deliverable.

Notes
-----

  - Found via the QEMU/binfmt cross-arch validation (Workstream B Phase 3).
    Reproduce on i386:  docker run --platform linux/386 <debian-gcc image>,
    build with CC=gcc, run tests/test_arithmetic against sources/libbocl.so;
    add CPPFLAGS=-D_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64 to see layer 2.


======================================================================
RESOLVED (v0.0.67)
======================================================================

There was only ONE real layer: the word-width discriminator. sources/kernel_types.h
selected `word` on `_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64`, which is defined on Debian armv7 but
NOT on i386, so armv7 got int64 `word` (correct) while i386 got int32 (fixnum
range ~2.1e9 -> integer arithmetic overflowed/wrong).

`word` is purely the fixnum integer type: there are no word<->pointer casts and
the TAG_MASK/TAGBODY_SHIFT macros are unused, so its width is independent of
pointer width. armv7 (32-bit pointers, int64 word) is the existence proof. Fix:
make `word` unconditionally int64 on every target. i386 now matches the 64-bit
baseline exactly (full unit suite 11 pass / 31 fail, test_arithmetic passes; no
test that passes on 64-bit fails on i386). 64-bit targets are unchanged.

The reported "layer 2" (wrong arithmetic even with int64 forced) was a
measurement ARTIFACT of the diagnosis, not a real defect: forcing the int64
branch via `CPPFLAGS=-D_POSIX_V6_LP64_OFF64` on only part of the build produced
a `word`-size ABI mismatch between libbocl and the test binary (one int32, one
int64), which is what corrupted the values. With the width changed consistently
in the header, no such failures occur. i386 is now a clean target.
